WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.800
- Thanks to Indochino for
keeping LegalEagle in the air

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:04.456
and helping me look fly.

00:00:04.456 --> 00:00:05.510
(eagle squawking)

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:07.410
Justice Brett Kavanaugh's first term

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.420
as Supreme Court justice
has come to a close

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.810
and it was a big one
for the Supreme Court.

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:15.140
To recap, Justice Anthony
Kennedy retired in 2018,

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.380
leaving a very important
vacancy with the court.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.780
Kennedy had been the court's swing vote.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.660
Lawyers often tailored their arguments

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.270
specifically to Kennedy

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:28.250
since he was capable of siding
with the court's Liberal wing

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:29.930
or the court's Conservatives

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:32.490
depending on the specific issue.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.930
There was wild speculation
as to whether the court

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.110
would start making a dramatic shift

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.630
now that it has a solid,
quote, Conservative majority,

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:43.770
and I'll note parenthetically
that the political alignments

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.990
of Conservative and Liberal
don't really hold up

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.610
when you're talking about
Supreme Court justices

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:51.950
but that's probably a
topic for another video.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:54.750
On first blush, it looks
like Chief Justice Roberts

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.340
may be taking up the mantle

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.200
of the High Court's
centrist and deciding vote.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.080
This term, the Supreme
Court has handed down

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.440
some big decisions on
partisan gerrymandering,

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.370
the census, profane trademarks, and more.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:09.570
Now that the Supreme
Court's 2019 term has ended,

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.527
let's take a look at the
most consequential decisions.

00:01:12.527 --> 00:01:15.870
(bright music)

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:16.730
Hey, LegalEagles!

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:18.370
Welcome back to Real Law Review

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:20.430
where today we're going to do a roundup

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.470
of what I consider the Supreme Court's

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:26.040
most consequential 2019 decisions.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:27.100
So let's dig right in.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:28.950
The first is the FUCT trademark,

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.700
which I am allowed to say on this channel

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.950
because it's spelled F-U-C-T,
not spelled any other way

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.600
so it's not a swear word.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.790
So starting with the FUCT trademark case

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:42.560
which invalidated a federal
law that prohibited, quote,

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.060
immoral or scandalous
trademarks, whatever that means,

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:47.900
which, of course, is kind of the point

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:50.350
of this particular Supreme Court case.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.590
For years, the US Patent and
Trademark Office or USPTO

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:55.100
has limited trademark protections

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.400
for things it deemed vulgar,
offensive, or scandalous

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.520
but in a victory for free
speech, the Supreme Court held

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.080
that the street clothing
called FUCT, F-U-C-T,

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.060
was entitled to federal
trademark protection.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.570
That issue was the Trademark Act of 1905

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:12.020
which prohibits the registration

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:14.670
of immoral and scandalous trademarks.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.220
However, under the First Amendment,

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.120
you can see where this is probably going,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.530
the government can't
penalize a person's speech

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:23.690
because it disagrees with the
viewpoint being expressed.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:27.700
Here, the viewpoint being swear words.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.590
The Supreme Court held that
it's impossible for the USPTO

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.740
to decide what it considered
scandalous or immoral

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:36.810
without resorting to
viewpoint discrimination.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:39.290
There is no coherent sense of these terms,

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.670
therefore the USPTO simply
applies its own sense

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:42.710
of what is right and wrong

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:44.720
and scandalous and what's not scandalous

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.580
which has some real
First Amendment problems.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:51.290
The 1905 law has led to some
bizarre decisions by the USPTO.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:53.750
For example, the government
granted a trademark

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:58.170
for the company that using
the word FCUK, F-C-U-K,

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:01.440
but denied it for FUCT, F-U-C-T,

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.420
even though both simply
play on the word (bleeps)

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.500
which we have to bleep because
that one is spelled F-U-C-K

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.980
because YouTube is more arbitrary

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.410
and capricious than the
USPTO at this point. (grunts)

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:16.480
Anyway, as a result,

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.880
a six-to-three majority
led by Justice Kagan

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.520
concluded that the prohibition
against the registration

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:22.850
of immoral or scandalous trademarks

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:23.960
violates the First Amendment.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.150
The majority ruled that
The Lanham Act's bars

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:27.850
were substantially over broad.

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:29.127
As Justice Kagan noted,

00:03:29.127 --> 00:03:30.617
"There are a great many immoral

00:03:30.617 --> 00:03:32.027
"and scandalous ideas in the world,

00:03:32.027 --> 00:03:33.603
"even more than there are swear words

00:03:33.603 --> 00:03:35.337
"and the Lanham Act should cover them all.

00:03:35.337 --> 00:03:37.117
"It therefore violates the First Amendment

00:03:37.117 --> 00:03:40.710
"to deny trademark protection
to those kind of marks."

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:42.930
The court made a similar ruling in 2017

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.160
when it ruled that a band named The Slants

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.610
had a right to trademark its name.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.920
The band used the term as
a way to reclaim a word

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.770
that is often used as
a slur against Asians.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.550
That case wound up protecting
the Washington Redskins

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:58.300
after the USPTO canceled
the team's trademarks

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.010
after complaints that the word Redskins

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.350
was a racial slur
against Native Americans.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:04.120
And while reasonable people can disagree

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:07.530
as to whether the term
Redskins is offensive or not,

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.417
we can all agree that we
should fire Dan Snyder,

00:04:10.417 --> 00:04:12.093
#FireDanSnyder, #HireDavidChang.

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:15.810
But back to the case at hand,

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.810
Justices Roberts, Breyer,
and Sotomayor dissented

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:22.570
although they agreed that the
word immoral is over broad,

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:24.630
they thought the ban on
scandalous trademarks

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.600
is constitutional but just needs

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:27.940
to be more narrowly tailored.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:29.140
The upshot of this decision

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:30.330
is that it's going to open the door

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:33.470
to all sorts of new and
interesting and profane trademarks

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:35.130
including many that probably

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:38.094
involve profanity in the
future, so (beeps) yeah.

00:04:38.094 --> 00:04:40.960
♪ America (bleeps) yeah ♪

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.070
- Racist jury selection.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:44.620
The court handed a big win

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.690
to advocates of unbiased jury
trial by ordering a new trial

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:50.040
for the death row inmate Curtis Flowers

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:53.180
who has been tried six times
over the past two decades.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:55.570
In the American justice
system of jury trials,

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:56.960
both sides get an opportunity

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.700
to strike a limited
number of jurors for cause

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.500
such as conflicts of interest.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.560
The parties also get to strike
a certain number of jurors

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:05.860
for any reason whatsoever.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.500
These are what are known
as peremptory challenges.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.630
There is one huge caveat, though.

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:12.830
The attorneys cannot use
their peremptory strikes

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:15.980
for racial reasons to
prevent potential jurors

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:18.410
from being selected because of their race.

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:20.530
This is called a Batson Challenge

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:22.240
which we've talked about
on this channel before.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.470
It goes back to the Supreme Court case

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:25.270
of Batson versus Mississippi.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:27.900
In the Flowers case,
district attorney Doug Evans

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:30.620
allegedly used peremptory
strikes to dismiss black jurors

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:34.390
from a jury pool in all
six of Flowers' trials.

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:38.520
Evans eliminated 41
out of 42 black jurors.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:41.640
The 72 majority opinion
written by Justice Kavanaugh

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.190
included that the state's pattern

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:45.630
of striking prospective black jurors

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:49.320
persisted from Flowers'
first trial through his sixth

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.510
and this was a relentless effort

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:54.380
to stop the jury from
having any black jurors

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:56.290
which strongly indicated that Evans

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:59.210
was trying to try Flowers

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:02.870
before the whitest jury he
could possibly find and create.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:04.940
The majority noted that
the prosecutors questioned

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:07.430
the black and white
prospective jurors differently.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:10.760
For example, they gave 145 questions

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:13.700
to just five perspective black jurors.

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:17.740
They asked only 12 questions
to the 11 white jurors seated

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:19.830
for Flowers' most recent trial.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:20.850
The prosecutor and his team

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:23.590
struck at least one
black prospective juror

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:26.070
who was similarly situated
to white prospective jurors

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:28.140
who were not struck by the state.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:30.690
So the Flowers case extends the doctrine

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:33.010
that was created in
Batson versus Mississippi

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:35.610
that you cannot use
your peremptory strikes

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:38.540
to strike all or most of the black jurors

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:41.360
from a prospective jury
pool which is exactly

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:45.180
what happened in the John
Grisham book and movie,

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:46.300
A Time to Kill.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.820
I'll put a link down to my
review in the description.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:51.700
The hotly-debated census citizenship case.

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:53.550
The US Constitution requires The US

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:55.610
to conduct a census every 10 years.

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:56.820
This is incredibly important

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.270
for political districting purposes.

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:01.620
The census counts every
person in the county

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.340
and is used to appropriate federal funds,

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:05.790
draw electoral districts,

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:08.700
and apportion representatives
to the various states.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:11.800
In March of 2018, Secretary
of Commerce Wilbur Ross

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.180
added a question to the 2020 census

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:15.150
which required each person

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:18.270
to state whether they
are a US citizen or not.

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.480
Ross claimed that the Trump administration

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:21.890
added the question because it was keen

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:24.230
to enforce Federal voting rights laws

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:26.560
to better protect minority
and voting rights.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:28.940
This rationale seemed pretty
suspect from the start

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:31.210
since the Trump administration
has never filed a lawsuit

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:32.870
to enforce the Voting Rights Act

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:35.330
and that rationale ran contrary

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:37.260
to a lot of the actual language

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:38.860
coming out of the Trump administration

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:40.490
and from President Trump himself.

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:42.560
The outcome of this case turned on

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:43.610
whether the Voting Rights Act

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:44.910
was really the government's motive

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:47.780
for asking the citizenship
question or not,

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:51.140
because generally, the courts
don't dispute the rationale

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:52.420
that the government puts forward.

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:54.390
But under the Administrative
Procedure Act,

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:56.100
any agency action that is, quote,

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.760
arbitrary or capricious violates the law.

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:01.690
Moreover at a minimum,
an agency must disclose

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:04.940
the basis of its action so
courts can review its legality.

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:06.900
Several states sued
the Commerce Department

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:10.780
and argued that the question
was arbitrary and capricious

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:12.710
and it was a move designed solely

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:15.260
to isolate and marginalize
people of color,

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:18.170
particularly Latinos
who may be undocumented

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:21.380
which would under count the
population in many US states,

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:22.810
particularly in the Southwest.

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:24.270
The plaintiffs feared that the question

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:28.040
would deter many Latinos from
answering the census question

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.090
because they would fear deportation.

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:32.440
The government argued it
was simply a return to form

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:35.060
because from 1820 to 1950,

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:37.460
the census included a
citizenship question.

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:38.500
The government also questioned

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:41.090
whether anyone had actual
standing to sue over the question

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:42.150
and said that the notion

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:44.900
that people would not answer
the question truthfully

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:46.350
was based on sheer speculation

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:48.680
because the question
hadn't been issued yet.

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:50.660
The plaintiffs contended that according

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:51.597
to the government's own records,

00:08:51.597 --> 00:08:54.600
the citizenship question
would lead to an under count

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.660
of at least five million people.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.390
This was the government's
conclusion after the 1950 census

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:02.320
which is why the government
dropped the question

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:03.153
in the first place.

00:09:03.153 --> 00:09:04.730
The plaintiffs prevailed
in the lower courts

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:06.590
which ruled that Ross's
actions were arbitrary

00:09:06.590 --> 00:09:08.400
and capricious based on pretext

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:11.140
and violated parts of the Census Act.

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:12.160
The district court concluded

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:14.050
that Ross had exceeded his authority

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:16.720
in crafting the question
because of its illegality.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:19.040
The Commerce Department
appealed to the Supreme Court.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:21.310
Now, the Supreme Court narrowly
sided with the plaintiffs

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:23.090
in a five-to-four decision

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:25.450
with chief justice casting the swing vote.

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:27.420
The court first overruled portions

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:28.940
of the lower court decision.

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:30.190
The majority of justices found

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:32.350
that Secretary Ross had the authority

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:33.620
to draft the census question

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:36.940
and was within his right as agency head

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:39.040
to seek records and data.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:41.870
However, Roberts concluded
that the rationale Ross gave

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:42.820
for the question,

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:45.730
wanting to help minorities
exercise their voting rights,

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:47.520
was quote contrived.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:50.180
Although agencies have
broad authority to regulate,

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:51.890
they must offer genuine reasons

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:53.280
for the decisions that they make.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:55.620
Secretary Ross's reason
for seeking the data

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:58.120
that he wanted to improve
voting rights access

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:01.130
appeared to play no actual
role in his decision.

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:03.470
In fact, Ross first
inquired about reinstating

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:04.540
the citizenship question

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:07.040
just one week after his appointment.

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:08.640
According to Justice Roberts,

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:10.180
altogether the evidence tells a story

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:12.400
that does not match the
secretary's explanation

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:13.470
for his decision.

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:14.303
In other words,

00:10:14.303 --> 00:10:17.630
Ross's reason for asking the
question was pre-textual.

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:18.870
The court ruled that for now,

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:20.770
the question could not go forward.

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:22.740
However, the opinion leaves the door open

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:24.310
for the government to
bring back the question

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:26.060
if they can come up with
another justification

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:28.700
for seeking that
information in the census,

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:31.130
which takes us to a similar
case with a different outcome,

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:32.900
that on partisan gerrymandering.

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:35.190
Partisan gerrymandering is
when one political party

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:38.320
draws the district maps at
the other party's expense,

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:41.530
making it easier for the party
in power to stay in power.

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:44.240
The States use census
data to draw up new maps

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:46.340
portioning representatives
to state legislators

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:47.440
in federal districts.

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:48.940
For many years, the political party

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:50.530
with the most power in the states

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:52.770
have drawn districts into strange shapes

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:55.520
that make it easier for
incumbents to hold on to power.

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:57.890
So can state officials ever go so far

00:10:57.890 --> 00:10:59.630
in drawing partisan districts

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:01.810
that it violates the US Constitution?

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:04.010
That was the question
before the Supreme Court

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:05.350
in this most recent case.

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:08.200
Justice Kennedy was the
swing vote back in 2004

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:11.550
when the Supreme Court's
Conservative justices plus Kennedy,

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:13.800
whether you consider
him Conservative or not,

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:15.770
ruled that the Supreme
Court should stay out

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:19.070
of a contentious gerrymandering
case from Pennsylvania.

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:21.200
However, Kennedy left the door ajar,

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:22.760
stating that in a future case,

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:25.290
it could be possible for
one party to skew maps

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.760
so much that it was unconstitutional.

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:29.580
The problem that Kennedy identified

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:31.620
was that there seemed to
be no workable standard

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:34.200
for determining when gerrymandering
was unconstitutional.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:36.140
However, with Kennedy's swing vote gone,

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:38.290
the court's Conservatives easily prevailed

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:40.490
in the two cases involving
partisan gerrymandering

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:43.220
in North Carolina and
Maryland during this term.

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:46.830
In a five-to-four decision
written by Chief Justice Roberts,

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:47.663
the Supreme court said

00:11:47.663 --> 00:11:49.930
that the Federal Courts
now lacked jurisdiction

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:52.530
to even hear partisan
gerrymandering cases.

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:54.827
Roberts declared partisan
gerrymandering as, quote,

00:11:54.827 --> 00:11:58.400
"Nothing new and a traditional
part of partisan politics."

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:00.840
Roberts and the other four
Conservative justices ruled

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:02.550
that the case was not justiciable

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:05.610
and that it runs afoul of the
Political Question Doctrine.

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:06.443
By the way, background,

00:12:06.443 --> 00:12:09.250
when a case presents a,
quote, political question,

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:11.800
Federal Courts will
refuse to hear the case.

00:12:11.800 --> 00:12:13.180
The legal doctrine was developed

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:15.490
to prevent Federal Courts
from intervening in disputes

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:17.200
that are so politically charged

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:20.470
that the courts feel that they
should just stay out of them

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:22.600
and let the legislatures deal

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:23.990
with those particular questions.

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:27.020
Here, the majority held that
partisan gerrymandering claims

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:28.180
present political questions

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:30.220
beyond the reach of the Federal Courts

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:31.740
which brings us to the court's opinion

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:33.310
in Gamble versus The United States,

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:35.940
dealing with the Dual
Sovereignty Doctrine.

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:37.460
The Dual Sovereignty Doctrine

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:40.070
is a pretty arcane piece of jurisprudence

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:41.550
but it generated lots of attention

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:42.580
because the media covered it

00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:44.630
as if it posed the following question.

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:46.900
Could a person connected to Donald Trump,

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:48.870
like convicted felon Paul Manafort

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:50.700
receive a pardon for federal charges

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:53.510
but still be prosecuted in the state court

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:54.940
for the same things?

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:56.560
However, the case pending before the court

00:12:56.560 --> 00:13:00.550
did not involve Manafort or
any Trump-related official

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:04.780
but it might have implications
into those individuals.

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:06.530
There's a legal doctrine which holds that

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:08.470
because the federal and state governments

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:10.290
are, quote, separate sovereigns,

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:12.450
the Double Jeopardy Clause does not apply

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:13.970
to prosecution of the same crime

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:15.800
under both federal and state laws.

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:18.490
This is known as the
Separate Sovereigns Doctrine.

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:21.530
So the question is can a
person wind up facing state

00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:24.060
and federal prosecutions
for the same conduct

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:26.020
or does that violate the
Double Jeopardy Clause

00:13:26.020 --> 00:13:26.853
of the Fifth Amendment?

00:13:26.853 --> 00:13:29.060
In the case at hand,
Terence Gamble was convicted

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:30.740
under Alabama state criminal law

00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:33.750
for being a felon in possession of a gun.

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:36.540
He was also prosecuted
under a federal criminal law

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:38.980
for being a felon in
possession of a gun as well.

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:40.710
He appealed his federal conviction

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:42.960
citing the Double Jeopardy Clause.

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:44.980
Gamble lost his appeal in the lower courts

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:47.160
and the Supreme Court considered the case.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:49.700
The Supreme Court's
seven-to-two majority decision

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:51.010
affirmed that Double Jeopardy

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:52.970
did not bar Gamble's conviction.

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:55.010
Justice Alito wrote the majority opinion

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:55.980
that there was no reason

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:58.730
to overturn the Dual Sovereignty Doctrine.

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:01.490
The Double Jeopardy Clause
bars successive prosecutions

00:14:01.490 --> 00:14:04.470
for the same offense,
not for the same conduct.

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:06.670
That means that Gamble
could not be tried twice

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:10.070
for the same violation of
the Alabama Criminal Codes

00:14:10.070 --> 00:14:11.900
but he could be tried again

00:14:11.900 --> 00:14:14.930
for arguably the same underlying conduct

00:14:14.930 --> 00:14:17.280
in both federal and state courts.

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:19.410
So while the majority of opinion
certainly will do nothing

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:22.500
to advance the cause of
criminal justice reform

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:26.090
and most defendants to be
tried by the Federal Government

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:28.330
and the state government will
probably just be regular Joes

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:29.650
like Terrence Gamble,

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:31.950
it does mean that any person Trump pardons

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:35.400
for a federal offense can
still be tried in state court.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:38.500
Unconscious and emergency
searches and seizures.

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:40.660
Another criminal justice
case from this term

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:43.080
placed some additional
limits on your rights

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:44.860
under the Fourth Amendment.

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:47.780
Six years ago, police found
Gerald Mitchell soaking wet

00:14:47.780 --> 00:14:50.850
and sloppy drunk in a
sandy beach in Wisconsin.

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:53.240
He was shirtless and slurring his words.

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:55.030
He took a preliminary breath test

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:56.700
which indicated his blood alcohol level

00:14:56.700 --> 00:14:58.690
was three times the legal limit.

00:14:58.690 --> 00:15:01.040
Since this test was ineligible
to be used in court,

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:03.330
police had to decide how
to get admissible evidence

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:04.960
of Mitchell's condition.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:07.580
They took him to a
hospital for a blood test.

00:15:07.580 --> 00:15:08.550
There was just one hitch.

00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:11.870
Mitchell passed out in the
backseat of the squad car

00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:12.870
on the way to the hospital

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:14.770
but the hospital staff drew his blood

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:15.940
while he was knocked out

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:19.970
which indicated a blood
alcohol level of 0.222.

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:22.250
The police never obtained a warrant.

00:15:22.250 --> 00:15:23.530
Mitchell tried to get the results

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:25.020
of the blood test thrown out

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:27.100
arguing that the cops needed a warrant

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:29.830
but Wisconsin state law
says the drivers on his road

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:32.430
presumably consent to a blood test.

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:34.480
The Supreme Court justices
ruled five to four

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:37.180
that the Fourth Amendment
generally does not stop states

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:39.710
from taking a blood
sample without a warrant

00:15:39.710 --> 00:15:42.170
if the suspect is unconscious.

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:44.340
The Fourth Amendment to the
Constitution protects your right

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:45.900
to unreasonable searches and seizures

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:47.720
and it states that both
the right of the people

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:51.100
to be secure in their persons,
houses, papers, and effects

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:52.810
against unreasonable searches and seizures

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:55.700
shall not be violated and
no warrants shall issue

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:57.690
but upon probable cause.

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:00.530
While the general rule is
that police need a warrant,

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:02.460
there are many exceptions to this rule.

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:03.430
One of the exceptions

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:06.100
is called the Exigent
Circumstances Exception

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:08.130
which lets police search without a warrant

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:11.130
to prevent the imminent
destruction of evidence.

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:13.040
Evidence of a person's blood alcohol level

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:14.340
disappears over time

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:16.730
as it is absorbed through the bloodstream.

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:18.800
The Supreme Court said
that if police waited

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:21.340
until an unconscious person is awake,

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:23.020
then the evidence that
they were intoxicated

00:16:23.020 --> 00:16:26.170
could be destroyed which allows police

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:29.950
to now draw blood from you
if you are unconscious,

00:16:29.950 --> 00:16:30.783
without a warrant.

00:16:30.783 --> 00:16:32.630
The Bladensburg Peace Cross.

00:16:32.630 --> 00:16:33.890
The Bladensburg Peace Cross

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:37.260
is a nearly century-old
monument in Maryland

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:39.550
to those who died in World War I.

00:16:39.550 --> 00:16:41.350
The cross sits on a traffic island

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:44.400
that is public land in
Bladensburg, Maryland.

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:47.030
The cross is associated with
Christianity, obviously,

00:16:47.030 --> 00:16:49.920
so the American Humanist
Association filed a lawsuit

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:52.490
alleging that the cross's
presence on public land

00:16:52.490 --> 00:16:55.310
violated the First Amendment's
Establishment Clause.

00:16:55.310 --> 00:16:56.660
The court's religion cases

00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:59.210
have recently given the
government wide latitude

00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:02.100
to include religious
references on state property

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:04.490
and material as long as they
can be branded as, quote,

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:06.970
ceremonial or celebratory
or commemorative.

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:08.210
For example, the court has ruled

00:17:08.210 --> 00:17:09.620
that the words In God We Trust

00:17:09.620 --> 00:17:11.440
should remain on the US currency

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:14.040
over the objection of non-theists.

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:15.690
The court reasoned that the message

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:17.170
is of historical importance

00:17:17.170 --> 00:17:19.980
and is now merely ceremonial
rather than religious.

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:22.730
The same is true of the
statement One Nation Under God,

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:24.160
another Christian reference,

00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:25.860
that the court says does not run afoul

00:17:25.860 --> 00:17:27.040
of the Establishment Clause.

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:29.407
Alito wrote, "A government
that roams the land,

00:17:29.407 --> 00:17:31.517
"tearing down monuments
with religious symbolism

00:17:31.517 --> 00:17:34.027
"and scrubbing away any
reference to the divine

00:17:34.027 --> 00:17:36.940
"will strike many as aggressively
hostile to religion."

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:39.750
Justices Ginsburg and Sotomayor dissented.

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:41.547
Ginsberg wrote, "Just as the Star of David

00:17:41.547 --> 00:17:43.077
"is not suitable to honor Christians

00:17:43.077 --> 00:17:44.337
"who died serving their country,

00:17:44.337 --> 00:17:47.547
"so a cross is not suitable
to honor those of other faiths

00:17:47.547 --> 00:17:49.770
"who died defending their nation."

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:51.420
The court appears to be drawing a line

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:52.690
between symbols and monuments

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:54.520
which have been in place for many years

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:57.050
and those which may be
erected in the future.

00:17:57.050 --> 00:17:59.670
Alito's argument probably
gave a little bit of life

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:03.070
to people who are angry about
the removal of old monuments.

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:04.700
However, commentators are split

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:06.900
on what that means for new monuments.

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:08.590
Now, if you're ever going to try a case

